  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe heaviest axis of precipitation across the southern\nmountains of Virginia and West Virginia continues to move out as\na cold front crosses this morning. Showers will still be likely\nacross the mountains through the morning with some residual\ndrizzle possible after sunrise. Patchy dense fog and low clouds\nare expected as well across the mountains, foothills, and river\nvalleys. The flood watch has been allowed to end as any\nadditional rainfall will be light with only 0.10\" and 0.20\" \nexpected across the mountains.\n\nDrier weather and mostly to partly sunny skies are in store\ntoday behind the front with breezy, comfortably warm, less humid \nconditions setting up in the afternoon. Saturday will be\nsimilar, but a quick moving disturbance will bring chances for\nafternoon showers and thunderstorms even as high pressure\nremains of influence. However, severe weather is not\nanticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA warm front will lift north Sunday bringing a return of the hot\nand humid conditions as well as showers and thunderstorms in \nthe evening. Building cloud cover may limit CAPE, but 40 to 50 \nknots of shear and lift from the front will likely create some \ninstances of organized convection early Sunday evening. Models\nare not in agreement with onset timing. The GFS and NAM show\narrival across the Mid-Ohio Valley by 6-8PM; the EURO has more \nof a SW to NE arrival with the coalfields and southern mountains\nseeing the first round of showers and thunderstorms. The\nCanadian model holds things back until late Sunday night/early\nMonday morning. \n\nHigher precipitable water values of around 2 inches or more\narrive early Monday morning leading to more productive rainfall\nrates with any showers and thunderstorms. The warm front is\nslated to park to our north leading to likely rounds showers \nand thunderstorms through the day. Moderate to heavy rainfall is\nexpected with any showers and storms on Monday. Between 1 and 2\ninches of rainfall is forecast across the area from Sunday night\nthrough Monday.\n\nThe stationary front will then move through the area Monday \nnight as a cold front. Some thunderstorms are possible along the\nfront. Drier conditions will return on Tuesday and part of\nWednesday. The heaviest axis is projected across our Ohio\ncounties and northern lowland counties of West Virginia where\nthe heaviest rain has fallen already the past few days.",
